"""The QR encoder, checked against known-good output rather than itself.

A wrong QR code is not a wrong number on a screen — it is a picture that
looks entirely correct and simply does not scan, discovered by someone
standing in a shop. So this file does not ask "does it still do what it did
last week". It asks "does it match the specification", three separate ways:

  * golden matrices generated by an independent reference implementation
    (the `qrcode` library, which is not a dependency of carraway — the
    vectors were taken once and frozen here),
  * structure the spec fixes regardless of content: finder patterns, timing
    patterns, the dark module, format information and its BCH check,
  * and a partial decode, which pulls the codewords back out of the finished
    grid and confirms the Reed-Solomon syndromes are zero.

That last one is the strongest of the three. It exercises the bitstream, the
error correction, the block interleaving and the module placement together,
and it would fail if any one of them drifted — including in ways a golden
vector taken at one length would not notice.
"""

import hashlib

import pytest

from carraway.ui import qr

# -- golden vectors ------------------------------------------------------
#
# Generated by the `qrcode` reference library at error-correction level M in
# byte mode, one mask pinned per case. Small enough to read as a picture: a
# failing diff shows you the code.

